{"qid":"adf60b28","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear Functions","difficulty":"easy","type":"mc","text":"<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\"><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 8<i>x</i> + 4</div>The function <i>f</i> gives the estimated height, in feet, of a willow tree <i>x</i> years after its height was first measured. Which statement is the best interpretation of 4 in this context?","choices":["The tree will be measured each year for 4 years.","The tree is estimated to grow to a maximum height of 4 feet.","The estimated height of the tree increased by 4 feet each year.","The estimated height of the tree was 4 feet when it was first measured."],"correct":3,"explanation":"It's given that the function <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = 8<i>x</i> + 4 gives the estimated height, in feet, of a willow tree <i>x</i> years after its height was first measured. For a function defined by an equation of the form <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>m</i><i>x</i> + <i>b</i>, where <i>m</i> and <i>b</i> are constants, <i>b</i> represents the value of <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) when <i>x</i> = 0. It follows that in the given function, 4 represents the value of <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) when <i>x</i> = 0. Therefore, the best interpretation of 4 in this context is that the estimated height of the tree was 4 feet when it was first measured.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors. Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors. Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual errors."},
{"qid": "cebcb853", "domain": "Algebra", "skill": "Equivalent Expressions", "difficulty": "easy", "type": "mc", "text": "<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\">(12<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + 5<i>x</i> &minus; 4) &minus; (7<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> &minus; 2<i>x</i> + 9)</div>Which expression is equivalent to the given expression?", "choices": ["5<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + 3<i>x</i> &minus; 13", "5<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + 3<i>x</i> + 5", "5<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + 7<i>x</i> &minus; 13", "5<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + 7<i>x</i> + 5"], "correct": 2, "explanation": "Choice C is correct. Subtracting the second expression means subtracting each of its terms. The <i>x</i><sup>3</sup> terms give 12<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> &minus; 7<i>x</i><sup>3</sup>, or 5<i>x</i><sup>3</sup>. The <i>x</i> terms give 5<i>x</i> &minus; (&minus;2<i>x</i>), or 7<i>x</i>. The constant terms give &minus;4 &minus; 9, or &minus;13. So the given expression is equivalent to 5<i>x</i><sup>3</sup> + 7<i>x</i> &minus; 13.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. This subtracts the constant term correctly but not the <i>x</i> term, giving 5<i>x</i> + (&minus;2<i>x</i>) rather than 5<i>x</i> &minus; (&minus;2<i>x</i>). Since &minus;(&minus;2<i>x</i>) is +2<i>x</i>, the <i>x</i> terms give 7<i>x</i>.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This subtracts only the first term of the second expression. The subtraction applies to all three of its terms, so the <i>x</i> terms give 5<i>x</i> + 2<i>x</i> and the constant terms give &minus;4 &minus; 9.<br><br>Choice D is incorrect. This subtracts the <i>x</i> term correctly but not the constant term, giving &minus;4 + 9 rather than &minus;4 &minus; 9. The constant terms give &minus;13."},
{"qid":"8452c42b","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Equivalent Expressions","difficulty":"easy","type":"mc","text":"Which expression is equivalent to 50<i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 5<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>?","choices":["250<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>","10<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>","45<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>","55<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Choice D is correct. The given expression shows addition of two like terms. Therefore, the given expression is equivalent to (50 + 5)<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, or 55<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. This expression is equivalent to (50)(5)<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, not (50 + 5)<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This expression is equivalent to <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">50</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">5</span></span><i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, not (50 + 5)<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect. This expression is equivalent to (50 − 5)<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>, not (50 + 5)<i>x</i><sup>2</sup>."},
{"qid": "4d828041", "domain": "Algebra", "skill": "Equivalent Expressions", "difficulty": "easy", "type": "mc", "text": "Which expression is equivalent to <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 3<i>x</i> &minus; 54?", "choices": ["(<i>x</i> &minus; 3)(<i>x</i> + 18)", "(<i>x</i> + 3)(<i>x</i> &minus; 18)", "(<i>x</i> + 9)(<i>x</i> &minus; 6)", "(<i>x</i> &minus; 9)(<i>x</i> + 6)"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "Choice D is correct. A quadratic expression of the form <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + b<i>x</i> + c factors as (<i>x</i> + m)(<i>x</i> + n), where m and n have a product of c and a sum of b. Here two numbers with a product of &minus;54 and a sum of &minus;3 are &minus;9 and 6, so the expression is equivalent to (<i>x</i> &minus; 9)(<i>x</i> + 6).<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. This expands to <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 15<i>x</i> &minus; 54. Although &minus;3 and 18 have a product of &minus;54, they have a sum of 15, not &minus;3.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This expands to <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> &minus; 15<i>x</i> &minus; 54. Although 3 and &minus;18 have a product of &minus;54, they have a sum of &minus;15, not &minus;3.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect. This expands to <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 3<i>x</i> &minus; 54. The two signs are interchanged: 9 and &minus;6 have a sum of 3, not &minus;3."},
{"qid":"d4d513ff","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Equivalent Expressions","difficulty":"easy","type":"mc","text":"Which expression is equivalent to 12<i>x</i> + 27?","choices":["12(9<i>x</i> + 1)","27(12<i>x</i> + 1)","3(4<i>x</i> + 9)","3(9<i>x</i> + 24)"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Choice C is correct. Each term in the given expression, 12<i>x</i> + 27, has a common factor of 3. Therefore, the expression can be rewritten as 3(4<i>x</i>) + 3(9), or 3(4<i>x</i> + 9). Thus, the expression 3(4<i>x</i> + 9) is equivalent to the given expression.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. This expression is equivalent to 108<i>x</i> + 12, not 12<i>x</i> + 27.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This expression is equivalent to 324<i>x</i> + 27, not 12<i>x</i> + 27.<br><br>Choice D is incorrect. This expression is equivalent to 27<i>x</i> + 72, not 12<i>x</i> + 27."},
{"qid":"7cb3a8ee","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Nonlinear Equations in One Variable and Systems of Equations in Two Variables","difficulty":"easy","type":"fr","text":"<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\">|<i>x</i> − 5| = 10</div>What is one possible solution to the given equation?","answer":"15","explanation":"The correct answer is 15 or −5. By the definition of absolute value, if |<i>x</i> − 5| = 10, then <i>x</i> − 5 = 10 or <i>x</i> − 5 = −10. Adding 5 to both sides of the first equation yields <i>x</i> = 15. Adding 5 to both sides of the second equation yields <i>x</i> = −5. Thus, the given equation has two possible solutions, 15 and −5. Note that 15 and -5 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer."},
{"qid":"db0107df","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Linear Equations in Two Variables","difficulty":"easy","type":"fr","text":"The <i>y</i>-intercept of the graph of 12<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = 18 in the <i>xy</i>-plane is (0, <i>y</i>). What is the value of <i>y</i>?","answer":"9","explanation":"It's given that the <i>y</i>-intercept of the graph of 12<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = 18 in the <i>xy</i>-plane is (0, <i>y</i>). Substituting 0 for <i>x</i> in the equation 12<i>x</i> + 2<i>y</i> = 18 yields 12(0) + 2<i>y</i> = 18, or 2<i>y</i> = 18. Dividing both sides of this equation by 2 yields <i>y</i> = 9. Therefore, the value of <i>y</i> is 9."},
{"qid":"aff28230","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Systems of Two Linear Equations in Two Variables","difficulty":"easy","type":"mc","text":"<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\"><i>x</i> = 10<br><i>y</i> = <i>x</i> + 21</div><div>The solution to the given system of equations is (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>). What is the value of <i>y</i>?</div>","choices":["2.1","10","21","31"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Choice D is correct. It's given by the first equation in the given system of equations that <i>x</i> = 10. Substituting 10 for <i>x</i> in the second equation in the given system yields <i>y</i> = 10 + 21, or <i>y</i> = 31. Therefore, the value of <i>y</i> is 31.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This is the value of <i>x</i>, not the value of <i>y</i>.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors."},
{"qid":"50774285","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Area and Volume","difficulty":"easy","type":"mc","text":"Each base of a right rectangular prism has a length of 19 inches and a width of 8 inches. The prism has a volume of 2,736 cubic inches. What is the height, in inches, of the prism?","choices":["18","27","144","152"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Choice A is correct. The volume, <i>V</i>, of a rectangular prism is given by the formula <i>V</i> = <i>ℓwh</i>, where <i>ℓ</i> is the length of the base, <i>w</i> is the width of the base, and <i>h</i> is the height of the prism. It's given that each base of a right rectangular prism has a length of 19 inches and a width of 8 inches, and that the prism has a volume of 2,736 cubic inches. Substituting 19 for <i>ℓ</i>, 8 for <i>w</i>, and 2,736 for <i>V</i> in the formula <i>V</i> = <i>ℓwh</i> gives 2,736 = (19)(8)(<i>h</i>), or 2,736 = 152<i>h</i>. Dividing each side of this equation by 152 yields 18 = <i>h</i>. Therefore, the height, in inches, of the prism is 18.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.<br><br>Choice D is incorrect. This is the area, in square inches, of the base of the prism, not the height, in inches, of the prism."},
{"qid":"802549ac","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Nonlinear Equations in One Variable and Systems of Equations in Two Variables","difficulty":"medium","type":"mc","text":"<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\">(<i>x</i> + 2)(<i>x</i> + 3) = (<i>x</i> − 2)(<i>x</i> − 3) + 10</div>Which of the following is a solution to the given equation?","choices":["1","0","−2","−5"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Choice A is correct. Applying the distributive property on the left- and right-hand sides of the given equation yields <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>x</i> + 6 = <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 2<i>x</i> − 3<i>x</i> + 6 + 10, or <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> + 5<i>x</i> + 6 = <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> − 5<i>x</i> + 16. Subtracting <i>x</i><sup>2</sup> from and adding 5<i>x</i> to both sides of this equation yields 10<i>x</i> + 6 = 16. Subtracting 6 from both sides of this equation and then dividing both sides by 10 yields <i>x</i> = 1.<br><br>Choices B, C, and D are incorrect. Substituting 0, −2, or −5 for <i>x</i> in the given equation will result in a false statement. If <i>x</i> = 0, the given equation becomes 6 = 16; if <i>x</i> = −2, the given equation becomes 0 = 30; and if <i>x</i> = −5, the given equation becomes 6 = 66. Therefore, the values 0, −2, and −5 aren't solutions to the given equation."},
{"qid":"ed92fb68","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Systems of Two Linear Equations in Two Variables","difficulty":"medium","type":"mc","text":"<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\">4<i>x</i> + 5<i>y</i> = 100<br>5<i>x</i> + 4<i>y</i> = 62</div><div>If the system of equations above has solution (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>), what is the value of <i>x</i> + <i>y</i>?</div>","choices":["0","9","18","38"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Choice C is correct. Adding the given equations yields 9<i>x</i> + 9<i>y</i> = 162. Dividing each side of the equation 9<i>x</i> + 9<i>y</i> = 162 by 9 gives <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> = 18.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect and may result from incorrectly adding the equations. Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or computational errors. Choice D is incorrect. This value is equivalent to <i>y</i> − <i>x</i>."},
{"qid": "4c6d5110", "domain": "Advanced Math", "skill": "Nonlinear Equations in One Variable and Systems of Equations in Two Variables", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "mc", "text": "The mean <i>m</i> of three quiz scores <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, and <i>c</i> is given by the equation <i>m</i> = <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i> + <i>b</i> + <i>c</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span></span>. Which equation correctly expresses <i>c</i> in terms of <i>m</i>, <i>a</i>, and <i>b</i>?", "choices": ["<i>c</i> = <i>m</i> − <i>a</i> − <i>b</i>", "<i>c</i> = <i>m</i> + <i>a</i> + <i>b</i>", "<i>c</i> = 3<i>m</i> + <i>a</i> + <i>b</i>", "<i>c</i> = 3<i>m</i> − <i>a</i> − <i>b</i>"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "Choice D is correct. Multiplying both sides of the given equation by 3 gives 3<i>m</i> = <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> + <i>c</i>. Subtracting <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> from both sides of this equation gives 3<i>m</i> − <i>a</i> − <i>b</i> = <i>c</i>, or <i>c</i> = 3<i>m</i> − <i>a</i> − <i>b</i>.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. This equation treats <i>m</i> as the sum of the three scores. But <i>m</i> is the mean of the scores, so the sum of the three scores is 3<i>m</i>, not <i>m</i>.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This equation treats <i>m</i> as the sum of the three scores, and it also adds <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> instead of subtracting them.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect. This equation adds <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> to 3<i>m</i> instead of subtracting them from 3<i>m</i>."},
{"qid":"05195d8a","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Percentages","difficulty":"medium","type":"fr","text":"In a sample, 80% of the items are faulty. There are 88 faulty items in the sample. How many total items are in the sample?","answer":"110","explanation":"The correct answer is 110. Let <i>x</i> represent the total number of items in the sample. It's given that 80% of the items are faulty and that there are 88 faulty items in the sample. Therefore, 80% of <i>x</i> is 88. Since 80% can be rewritten as 80/100, it follows that (80/100)<i>x</i> = 88. Multiplying both sides of this equation by 100 yields 80<i>x</i> = 8,800. Dividing both sides of this equation by 80 yields <i>x</i> = 110. Therefore, there are 110 total items in the sample."},
{"qid":"fd8745fc","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Lines, Angles, and Triangles","difficulty":"medium","type":"fr","text":"In triangle <i>JKL</i>, the measures of ∠<i>K</i> and ∠<i>L</i> are each 48°. What is the measure of ∠<i>J</i>, in degrees? (Disregard the degree symbol when entering your answer.)","answer":"84","explanation":"The correct answer is 84. The sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is 180°. It's given that in triangle <i>JKL</i>, the measures of ∠<i>K</i> and ∠<i>L</i> are each 48°. Adding the measures, in degrees, of ∠<i>K</i> and ∠<i>L</i> gives 48 + 48, or 96. Therefore, the measure of ∠<i>J</i>, in degrees, is 180 − 96, or 84."},
{"qid": "75c15b42", "domain": "Algebra", "skill": "Linear Equations in Two Variables", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "mc", "text": "The graph of the equation <i>ax</i> + <i>by</i> = <i>c</i> in the <i>xy</i>-plane is a line that passes through the points (&minus;3, 8) and (6, 2), where <i>a</i>, <i>b</i>, and <i>c</i> are nonzero constants. What is the value of <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span></span>?", "choices": ["&minus;<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span></span>", "&minus;<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span></span>", "<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span></span>", "<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span></span>"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "Choice D is correct. Since both points lie on the line, substituting each point into the equation gives &minus;3<i>a</i> + 8<i>b</i> = <i>c</i> and 6<i>a</i> + 2<i>b</i> = <i>c</i>. The two left sides are both equal to <i>c</i>, so &minus;3<i>a</i> + 8<i>b</i> = 6<i>a</i> + 2<i>b</i>. Subtracting 2<i>b</i> and adding 3<i>a</i> to both sides gives 6<i>b</i> = 9<i>a</i>, and dividing both sides by 6<i>a</i> gives <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span></span> = <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">6</span></span>, or <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span></span>. (The constants themselves cannot be determined: 2<i>x</i> + 3<i>y</i> = 18 and 4<i>x</i> + 6<i>y</i> = 36 both pass through the two points. Alternatively, the slope of the line is <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2 &minus; 8</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">6 &minus; (&minus;3)</span></span>, or &minus;<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span></span>, and the slope of the graph of <i>ax</i> + <i>by</i> = <i>c</i> is &minus;<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span></span>, so <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span></span> = <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span></span> and <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span></span> = <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span></span>.)<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. This is the reciprocal of the slope of the line, which is &minus;<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span></span>, not <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span></span>.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This is the slope of the line, which is equal to &minus;<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span></span>, not <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span></span>.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect. This is the value of <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span></span>, not <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>b</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>a</i></span></span>."},
{"qid": "2aedce1f", "domain": "Advanced Math", "skill": "Nonlinear Functions", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "mc", "text": "<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\"><i>f</i>(<i>x</i>) = <i>a</i>(5)<sup><i>x</i></sup></div>In the given function, <i>a</i> is a constant. If <i>f</i>(2) = 175, what is the value of <i>a</i>?", "choices": ["7", "35", "150", "173"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Choice A is correct. Substituting 2 for <i>x</i> in the given function gives <i>f</i>(2) = <i>a</i>(5)<sup>2</sup>. Since (5)<sup>2</sup> = 25, this is 25<i>a</i>. It's given that <i>f</i>(2) = 175, so 25<i>a</i> = 175. Dividing both sides of this equation by 25 yields <i>a</i> = 7.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. This is <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">175</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">5</span></span>, which divides by 5 rather than by 5<sup>2</sup>. Substituting 2 for <i>x</i> gives <i>a</i>(5)<sup>2</sup>, not <i>a</i>(5).<br><br>Choice C is incorrect. This is 175 &minus; 5<sup>2</sup>, which subtracts 25 from 175. In the given function <i>a</i> is multiplied by 5<sup><i>x</i></sup>, so 175 must be divided by 25.<br><br>Choice D is incorrect. This is 175 &minus; 2, which subtracts the input from 175. The input 2 is the exponent of 5, not a quantity subtracted from <i>a</i>."},
{"qid":"b4f5a7ca","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"Evaluating Statistical Claims: Observational Studies and Experiments","difficulty":"medium","type":"mc","text":"A survey was conducted using a sample of history professors selected at random from the California State Universities. The professors surveyed were asked to name the publishers of their current texts. What is the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized?","choices":["All professors in the United States","All history professors in the United States","All history professors at all California State Universities","All professors at all California State Universities"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Choice C is correct. Selecting a sample at random when conducting a survey allows the results to be generalized to the population from which the sample was selected, but not beyond this population. In this situation, the population that the sample was selected from is history professors from the California State Universities. Therefore, the largest population to which the results of the survey can be generalized is all history professors at all California State Universities.<br><br>Choices A, B, and D are incorrect. Since the sample was selected at random from history professors from the California State Universities, the results of the survey can't be generalized to all professors in the United States, all history professors in the United States, or all professors at all California State Universities. All three of these populations may use different texts and therefore may name different publishers."},
{"qid": "1b4c4113", "domain": "Geometry and Trigonometry", "skill": "Area and Volume", "difficulty": "medium", "type": "mc", "text": "<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0;\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 280 225\" class=\"dx-fig\" role=\"img\" style=\"color:var(--text);max-width:255px;\"><title>A five-sided region made of a rectangle with a triangle sitting on its top side. The bottom of the rectangle is labeled 22 and the left side of the rectangle is labeled 13. A dashed segment runs from the top point of the triangle straight down to the bottom side, meeting it at a marked right angle, . The top of the rectangle is shown as a dashed segment</title><polygon points=\"60,190 210,190 210,130 135,80 60,130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"130\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"135\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"190\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><polyline points=\"135.0,180.0 145.0,180.0 145.0,190.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"208\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"currentColor\">22</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"164\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\">13</text></svg></div>Note: Figure not drawn to scale.<br><br>The region shown is formed by a rectangle and a triangle. The lengths shown are in centimeters, and the dashed segment from the top point of the triangle to the bottom of the rectangle is 21 centimeters long. What is the area, in square centimeters, of the region?", "choices": ["286", "374", "429", "517"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Choice B is correct. The region is made of a rectangle and a triangle, so its area is the sum of their areas. The figure shows that the rectangle is 22 centimeters wide and 13 centimeters tall, so its area is 22(13), or 286 square centimeters. The dashed segment is 21 centimeters long and is perpendicular to the base, and 13 centimeters of it is the height of the rectangle, so the height of the triangle is (21 − 13), or 8, centimeters. The base of the triangle is the top side of the rectangle, which is 22 centimeters long, so the area of the triangle is <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">1</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">2</span></span>(22)(8), or 88 square centimeters. Therefore, the area of the region is 286 + 88, or 374, square centimeters.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. This is the area of the rectangle alone, 22(13). It leaves out the triangle.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect. This uses 13, the height of the rectangle, as the height of the triangle. The height of the triangle is the part of the 21-centimeter segment above the rectangle.<br><br>Choice D is incorrect. This uses 21, the height of the whole region, as the height of the triangle. Only the part of that segment above the rectangle is the height of the triangle."},
{"qid":"f03465dc","domain":"Algebra","skill":"Systems of Two Linear Equations in Two Variables","difficulty":"hard","type":"mc","text":"<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0; font-size:1.1em;\">8<i>x</i> + 7<i>y</i> = 9<br>24<i>x</i> + 21<i>y</i> = 27</div><div>For each real number <i>r</i>, which of the following points lies on the graph of each equation in the <i>xy</i>-plane for the given system?</div>","choices":["(<i>r</i>, −<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8<i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span> + <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span>)","(−<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8<i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span> + <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span>, <i>r</i>)","(−<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8<i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span> + 9, <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8<i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span> + 27)","(<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span></span> + 9, −<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\"><i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">3</span></span> + 27)"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Choice A is correct. Dividing both sides of the second equation in the given system by 3 yields 8<i>x</i> + 7<i>y</i> = 9, which is the first equation in the given system. Therefore, the first and second equations represent the same line in the <i>xy</i>-plane. If the <i>x</i>- and <i>y</i>-coordinates of a point satisfy an equation, the point lies on the graph of the equation in the <i>xy</i>-plane. Choice A is a point with <i>x</i>-coordinate <i>r</i> and <i>y</i>-coordinate −<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8<i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span> + <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span>. Substituting <i>r</i> for <i>x</i> and −<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8<i>r</i></span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span> + <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span> for <i>y</i> in the equation 8<i>x</i> + 7<i>y</i> = 9 yields 8<i>r</i> + 7(−<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span><i>r</i> + <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span>) = 9. Applying the distributive property to the left-hand side of this equation yields 8<i>r</i> − 8<i>r</i> + 9 = 9. Combining like terms on the left-hand side of this equation yields 9 = 9, so the coordinates of the point (<i>r</i>, −<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span><i>r</i> + <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span>) satisfy both equations in the given system. Therefore, for each real number <i>r</i>, the point (<i>r</i>, −<span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">8</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span><i>r</i> + <span style=\"display:inline-flex;flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;vertical-align:middle;line-height:1.15;margin:0 0.12em;font-size:0.95em;\"><span style=\"border-bottom:1px solid currentColor;padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">9</span><span style=\"padding:0 0.25em;text-align:center;white-space:nowrap;\">7</span></span>) lies on the graph of each equation in the <i>xy</i>-plane for the given system.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.<br><br>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.<br><br>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors."},
{"qid":"6b4707aa","domain":"Geometry and Trigonometry","skill":"Circles","difficulty":"hard","type":"fr","text":"Circle <i>A</i> in the <i>xy</i>-plane has the equation (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 25. Circle <i>B</i> has the same center as circle <i>A</i>. The radius of circle <i>B</i> is two times the radius of circle <i>A</i>. The equation defining circle <i>B</i> in the <i>xy</i>-plane is (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = <i>k</i>, where <i>k</i> is a constant. What is the value of <i>k</i>?","answer":"100","explanation":"The correct answer is 100. An equation of a circle in the <i>xy</i>-plane can be written as (<i>x</i> − <i>t</i>)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − <i>u</i>)<sup>2</sup> = <i>r</i><sup>2</sup>, where the center of the circle is (<i>t</i>, <i>u</i>), the radius of the circle is <i>r</i>, and where <i>t</i>, <i>u</i>, and <i>r</i> are constants. It’s given that the equation of circle <i>A</i> is (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 25, which is equivalent to (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 5<sup>2</sup>. Therefore, the center of circle <i>A</i> is (−5, 5) and the radius of circle <i>A</i> is 5. It’s given that circle <i>B</i> has the same center as circle <i>A</i> and that the radius of circle <i>B</i> is two times the radius of circle <i>A</i>. Therefore, the center of circle <i>B</i> is (−5, 5) and the radius of circle <i>B</i> is 2(5), or 10. Substituting −5 for <i>t</i>, 5 for <i>u</i>, and 10 for <i>r</i> in the equation (<i>x</i> − <i>t</i>)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − <i>u</i>)<sup>2</sup> = <i>r</i><sup>2</sup> yields (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup>, which is equivalent to (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 100. It follows that the equation of circle <i>B</i> in the <i>xy</i>-plane is (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = 100. It’s also given that the equation defining circle <i>B</i> in the <i>xy</i>-plane is (<i>x</i> + 5)<sup>2</sup> + (<i>y</i> − 5)<sup>2</sup> = <i>k</i>, where <i>k</i> is a constant. Therefore, the value of <i>k</i> is 100."},
{"qid":"58dcc59f","domain":"Advanced Math","skill":"Nonlinear Functions","difficulty":"hard","type":"fr","text":"A landscaper is designing a rectangular garden. The length of the garden is to be 5 feet longer than the width. If the area of the garden will be 104 square feet, what will be the length, in feet, of the garden?","answer":"13","explanation":"Let <i>w</i> represent the width of the rectangular garden, in feet. Since the length of the garden will be 5 feet longer than the width, the length will be <i>w</i> + 5 feet. Thus the area will be <i>w</i>(<i>w</i> + 5). It's given that the area will be 104 square feet, so <i>w</i>(<i>w</i> + 5) = 104, which is equivalent to <i>w</i><sup>2</sup> + 5<i>w</i> − 104 = 0. Factoring this equation results in (<i>w</i> + 13)(<i>w</i> − 8) = 0. Therefore, <i>w</i> = 8 or <i>w</i> = −13. Because width cannot be negative, the width of the garden must be 8 feet. This means the length of the garden must be 8 + 5 = 13 feet."},
{"qid":"d3b9c8d8","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill":"One-Variable Data: Distributions and Measures of Center and Spread","difficulty":"hard","type":"mc","text":"<div style=\"text-align:center; margin:1.2em 0;\"><img src=\"question-bank-assets/d3b9c8d8.png\" alt=\"Box plots comparing the masses in kilograms of Group 1 and Group 2 gazelles, on a number line from 20 to 32\" style=\"max-width:280px;width:100%;height:auto;\"/></div>The box plots summarize the masses, in kilograms, of two groups of gazelles. Based on the box plots, which of the following statements must be true?","choices":["The mean mass of group 1 is greater than the mean mass of group 2.","The mean mass of group 1 is less than the mean mass of group 2.","The median mass of group 1 is greater than the median mass of group 2.","The median mass of group 1 is less than the median mass of group 2."],"correct":2,"explanation":"Choice C is correct. The median of a data set represented in a box plot is represented by the vertical line within the box. It follows that the median mass of the gazelles in group 1 is 25 kilograms, and the median mass of the gazelles in group 2 is 24 kilograms. Since 25 kilograms is greater than 24 kilograms, the median mass of group 1 is greater than the median mass of group 2.<br><br>Choice A is incorrect. The mean mass of each of the two groups cannot be determined from the box plots.<br><br>Choice B is incorrect. The mean mass of each of the two groups cannot be determined from the box plots.<br><br>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors."}
